[{:db/id -1 :adr/id 2799007214 :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7214 — Structural Metal Preparers and Erectors Job-Site Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage" :adr/status "accepted" :adr/doc_type "adr" :adr/date "2026-07-18" :adr/source-format "edn" :adr/scope ["cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7214" "kotoba-lang/occupation"] :adr/references [2607121000] :adr/related ["adr-2607121000-cloud-itonami-global-isic-isco-reverse-toposort-plan" "adr-2799007121-cloud-itonami-isco-7121-roofers-coverage" "adr-2799007131-cloud-itonami-isco-7131-painters-and-related-workers-coverage"] :registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit] :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger] :registry/maturity :implemented :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7214" :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7214" :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7214 :actor/name "Structural Steel Erection Crew Scheduling & Logistics Coordination Service" :actor/isco-08 "7214" :actor/wave 3 :actor/domain :construction/structural-steel-erection-coordination :actor/robotics? true :actor/coordination? true :actor/proposal-ops [:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order] :actor/hard-checks [:no-site :no-actuation :unknown-op :site-mismatch :unknown-worker :worker-wrong-site :scope-exclusion-violation] :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-safety-concern] :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:finalize-structural-steel-erection-execution :execute-structural-steel-erection-directly :perform-structural-steel-erection-directly :authorize-crane-lift :override-site-safety-officer-judgment :bypass-site-safety-officer-signoff] :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["proceed with the structural-steel erection" "proceed with the structural steel erection work" "finalize the structural-steel-erection-execution decision" "finalize the structural steel erection execution decision" "execute the structural-steel erection directly" "execute the structural steel erection directly" "perform the structural steel erection directly" "dispatch the crew to perform structural steel erection" "authorize the crane lift" "authorize the crane-lift" "override the site safety officer's judgment" "override the site-safety officer" "bypass the site-safety officer" "bypass crane-lift authorization"] :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 15000}" :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold] :operation/entry-point :intake :test/simulator :steelcoord.actor-test :test/counts "{:total 24, :assertions 59, :failures 0, :errors 0}" :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :site-verification :worker-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-order-cost-threshold-escalation :always-escalate-safety-concern :crane-lift-authorization-scope-exclusion] :adr/body "# ADR 2799007214: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7214 Structural Metal Preparers and Erectors job-site scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7214\n-- Structural Metal Preparers and Erectors** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3\nbatches 1-2 of ~17 already landed:\n7111/7112/7113/7114/7119/7121/7122/7123/7124/7125/7127/7131).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7214\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7214\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Structural Metal Preparers and\nErectors\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and\n`:operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and\nno `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7214` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7214` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This actor carries a high-steel/structural-erection safety\ndimension: structural metal preparers and erectors assemble\nsteel-frame structures -- one of the highest fall-risk/crane-hazard\ntrades (structural steel erection at height, crane-load handling).**\nA wrongful automated decision here could directly endanger a worker's\nlife. The closed op allowlist therefore must NEVER include any op\nthat directly finalizes a structural-steel-erection-execution\ndecision, authorizes a crane-lift, or overrides a site-safety\nofficer's judgment -- such a decision must always be a hard,\npermanent block or an always-escalate op, never auto-commit-eligible.\nThis is the same shape the `cloud-itonami-isco-7121` (Roofers)\nprecedent established for its own fall-risk trade, and this ADR\napplies it to structural steel erection's additional crane-lift\nhazard dimension.\n\n**This actor coordinates JOB-SITE SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY -- it\nnever performs structural-steel erection work itself.** It is not an\nauthority to decide when or how erection work happens on a job site;\nit is a coordination layer for work-record logging, crew/crane\nscheduling, safety-concern surfacing and materials-order\ncoordination, all gated behind an independent governor.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern\nis\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7121`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7121)\n(Roofers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph, full doc set, same wave and a similar height-work safety\npattern), cloned and read in full this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7214` as an OSS\ndocumentation/coordination actor for structural metal preparers and\nerectors, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Steel Erection\nCoordination Advisor ⊣ SteelCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library\ncoordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger. All source is portable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests\nare `clojure.test` `.clj`, matching the reference repo's own layout.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task/crane-schedule scheduling\n  proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a fall-hazard/crane-condition/\n  load-hazard concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- structural-steel-materials\n  procurement proposal, escalates above a registered cost threshold\n\nNo op in this allowlist finalizes a structural-steel-erection-\nexecution decision, authorizes a crane lift, or overrides a\nsite-safety officer's judgment -- these decision classes are\nstructurally absent from the allowlist, not merely gated behind\nescalation.\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (`steelcoord.governor`, permanent, never\noverridable by human approval)\n\n1. **no-site** -- the job site named by the request must be\n   independently verified/registered before any action.\n2. **no-actuation** -- proposal `:effect` must be `:propose` (the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never performs\n   structural-steel erection work).\n3. **unknown-op** -- `:op` must be one of the four allowlisted\n   coordination ops; the closed op-allowlist is enforced\n   unconditionally.\n4. **site-mismatch** -- if the proposal names a site, it must be the\n   SAME site verified for this request (defense-in-depth against a\n   proposal quietly targeting an unverified site).\n5. **unknown-worker / worker-wrong-site** -- if the proposal\n   references a worker, that worker must be a REGISTERED crew member\n   belonging to this site.\n6. **scope-exclusion-violation** -- a proposal that attempts, in any\n   free-text field, to finalize a structural-steel-erection-execution\n   decision, to authorize a crane lift, or to override a site-safety\n   officer's judgment is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated\n   unconditionally on content, independent of `:op`.\n\n### Escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (`steelcoord.governor/always-escalate-ops`). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a fall-hazard/\n  crane-condition/load-hazard observation -- it never resolves the\n  concern itself.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-order-cost-threshold` (15000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-or-below-cost-threshold`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence.\n- advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` (0.6) also escalates,\n  for any op.\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare nouns (e.g.\n\"steel\", \"crane\", \"beam\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this risk is especially\nsharp here because this actor's ENTIRE domain is structural-steel\nerection job sites at height with crane-load handling, so bare nouns\nlike \"steel\"/\"crane\"/\"beam\" are ordinary, unavoidable vocabulary.\n`steelcoord.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` is phrased exclusively\nas finalization/execution ACTION PHRASES (e.g. \"proceed with the\nstructural-steel erection\", \"authorize the crane lift\", \"override the\nsite safety officer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked\nexample phrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`steelcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor (including a\n`:flag-safety-concern` request whose description names a fall hazard\n\"near an open edge at height\" and an unsecured load \"near the crane\npath\"), never trips the scope-exclusion guard. A companion test,\n`scope-exclusion-not-triggered-by-bare-domain-nouns`, asserts directly\nagainst `governor/scope-exclusion-violation?` that a rationale/\ndescription containing only bare domain nouns (\"structural steel job\nsite ... at height near the crane ... fall-protection equipment and\ncrane signal procedures for this steel frame\") does not trip the\nguard, while\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-structural-steel-erection`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-authorize-crane-lift` and\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-safety-officer`\nconfirm the actual forbidden action phrases DO trip it, and\n`holds-a-scope-excluded-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path` /\n`holds-a-crane-lift-authorization-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nconfirm the full actor graph resolves such attempts straight to\n`:hold` (a finish point, never `:request-approval`) with no record\ncommitted.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7214\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7214` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7214`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 24 tests, 59 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a fresh clone of the pushed repository.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-7121`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-crane-lift` that is merely\n  gated behind `always-escalate-ops`: rejected outright per the\n  task's explicit high-steel/structural-erection safety guidance --\n  an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to authorize a\n  crane lift exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a\n  human gate. For a decision that can directly endanger a worker's\n  life during a crane lift or steel-erection sequence, the capability\n  itself must not exist in the allowlist at all.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"steel\" \"crane\"\n  \"beam\"]`): rejected per the fleet-known self-trip bug class --\n  this actor's entire domain is structural-steel erection job sites\n  at height with crane-load handling, so bare nouns would self-trip\n  on essentially every legitimate proposal (materials orders naming\n  \"structural steel W-beams\", crew-schedule proposals naming \"stage\n  crane for column set\", safety flags naming a hazard \"near the crane\n  path\", etc.).\n- Checking site/worker provenance only from the proposal (never the\n  request): rejected -- the site-mismatch check requires the site\n  identity to be anchored at the request level and independently\n  cross-checked against anything the proposal claims, so a proposal\n  that quietly names a different, unverified site is caught even if\n  the proposal otherwise looks well-formed.\n- Modeling the worker-registration check as unconditionally required\n  on every op: rejected as too rigid -- `:coordinate-supply-order`\n  legitimately has no worker to reference (it is a materials-\n  procurement proposal against the site, not a person); the check\n  applies only when a proposal actually references a `:worker-id`.\n- Folding the crane-lift-authorization hazard into the same generic\n  \"execution\" phrase family used by the roofers reference (no\n  crane-specific phrases): rejected -- structural steel erection's\n  crane-load-handling hazard is materially distinct from a generic\n  \"execute the work directly\" phrase (a proposal could name a crane\n  lift specifically without using generic execution language), so\n  dedicated crane-lift-authorization phrases (\"authorize the crane\n  lift\", \"authorize the crane-lift\", \"bypass crane-lift\n  authorization\") and a dedicated regression test\n  (`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-authorize-crane-lift`,\n  `holds-a-crane-lift-authorization-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`)\n  were added independently of the generic erection-execution phrases.\n"}]
